# Skin-Swing R&B

**Ingredients:** OKINAWAN SANSHIN + NEW JACK SWING / SWINGBEAT R&B + DX7-STYLE ELECTRIC PIANO + SYNTH BASS + RIMSHOT / CLAP + BASS CLARINET

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a winter residency in a converted ferry-ticket annex in Naha, where sanshin player Emi Arakaki was rehearsing clipped three-string phrases while Newark producer Marcus Vale rebuilt late-80s swingbeat drum patterns from punchy kicks, rimshots, claps, gated snare tails, and tightly quantized synth bass. Their first attempt sounded like ordinary new jack swing with a traditional plucked lead pasted over it. The useful discovery came when Marcus soloed the sanshin microphone and noticed that the snakeskin resonator gave every plectrum attack a short percussive snap before the pitch fully bloomed. He began aligning those attacks not with the obvious backbeat, but with the swung sixteenth pickups immediately before it. The acoustic transient became a human pre-echo to the machine clap. Emi then reduced her playing to compact two- and three-note cells, stopped using continuous strumming, and treated left-hand slides as rhythmic delays rather than ornaments. The result became a form where the sanshin physically pulls the programmed groove forward by a fraction of a beat.

Skin-Swing R&B is a dry, rhythm-centered acoustic-electronic style built from Okinawan sanshin articulation and late-1980s / early-1990s new jack swing. It normally runs between 96 and 112 BPM in 4/4, with programmed kick, rimshot or snare, close clap, swung sixteenth hats, short synth-bass notes, glassy electric-piano chords, restrained vocal stacks, and a real sanshin occupying the upper-mid rhythmic pocket.

The core aesthetic is **acoustic pickup against machine backbeat**. The groove does not depend on making the sanshin sound futuristic or heavily processed. Instead, its raw plectrum transient and snakeskin body resonance are used as timing information. A short attack placed a swung sixteenth before the clap can make the entire beat feel more elastic without moving the machine events themselves.

The sanshin is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser aimed between bridge and body edge, one ribbon microphone 50–80 cm back for body warmth, and an optional contact pickup used only for muted strokes and nail noise. The close microphone remains relatively dry. The ribbon may receive a compact room or plate. Heavy compression is avoided because the difference between hard plectrum strike, soft brush, muted rake, and sliding sustain is structurally important.

Players favor tonic-fifth shapes, open-string drones, minor thirds, major seconds, fourths, flat sevenths, pentatonic fragments, and short modal cells. Dense chordal strumming is avoided. Most phrases contain only two to five attacks. Slides are short—usually a semitone or whole tone—and timed so the destination pitch lands on or just after a machine accent.

The primary compositional device is the **Skin Pickup**. A sanshin note is struck one swung sixteenth before a snare, clap, kick, or bass event. The acoustic attack acts as a physical pickup into the machine hit. The note may decay before the backbeat arrives or continue slightly across it, but the initial transient must remain clearly ahead.

A second device, **Clap Shadow**, removes one programmed clap from a repeating pattern and replaces its expected presence with a strong muted sanshin rake or dead-string snap. The listener feels the missing clap because the acoustic transient occupies nearly the same spectral region while sounding visibly hand-played.

A third device, **Slide Lock**, begins a sanshin slide before the backbeat and reaches the target pitch exactly when the snare or bass lands. The rhythmic event is therefore divided into two phases: physical string motion before the beat, tonal arrival on the beat.

A fourth device, **Three-String Relay**, assigns different functions to the three strings. The lowest string carries tonic or fifth pickups, the middle string carries chord-color notes, and the upper string supplies short offbeat answers or deadened scratches. The player can change which string owns each role between sections without changing the harmony.

A fifth device, **Body Break**, removes synth bass and electric piano for one or two bars while drums and sanshin continue. The resonator skin, plectrum attack, and muted body noises must preserve enough rhythmic information that the song still feels like swingbeat rather than a folk interlude.

New jack swing drums remain compact, punchy, and highly controlled. Kick is firm and short. Snare or rimshot is dry, sometimes reinforced with a gated room tail no longer than necessary. Claps stay narrow and close. Hats use swung sixteenths with clear velocity hierarchy. Percussion fills are short and geometric rather than trap-like or festival-oriented.

Synth bass uses short, rubbery notes with quick release. Lines favor tonic, fifth, flat seventh, octave, chromatic approach notes, and syncopated anticipations. Long sub tails are avoided. During Skin Pickup passages, the bass often lands after the sanshin transient rather than doubling it.

DX7-style or FM electric piano supplies the harmonic chassis. Voicings favor minor 7, major 7, dominant 9, sus2, add9, sixth chords, and compact slash-chord colors. Chords are short enough to leave air around the sanshin. Bright bell-like FM patches are avoided; the tone should remain soft, glassy, and mid-forward.

Bass clarinet supplies low acoustic color through chalumeau stabs, breath-heavy pedals, tongue stops, and short falling intervals. It appears mainly in Body Break sections or after long vocal phrases, where another synth layer would make the mix too polished.

Vocals are encouraged but restrained. Okinawan Japanese, standard Japanese, and English can coexist in short phrases. Delivery should be intimate and rhythmic: low-register verses, dry unison hooks, breathy doubles, short stacked responses, and spoken pickups. Large gospel stacks and power-ballad belting are avoided.

Effects remain period-aware without becoming parody. Sanshin receives short room or plate, tiny slap delay only on selected phrase endings, and mild transformer saturation. FM keys may use subtle chorus. Vocals can use plate and very short delay throws. Drums remain mostly dry. Avoid synthwave pads, EDM risers, trap rolls, cinematic Okinawan textures, fake shamisen libraries, giant reverb, and decorative sanshin intros disconnected from the groove.

The defining tension is between a human acoustic transient arriving slightly early and a machine backbeat arriving exactly where expected.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 96–112 BPM, 4/4 new jack swing / swingbeat with punchy kick, dry rimshot or snare, close clap, swung sixteenth hats, short synth bass, and sanshin attacks placed as acoustic pickups around the backbeat.

**Harmonic Palette:** Minor 7, major 7, dominant 9, sus2, add9, sixth chords, open fifths, tonic drones, pentatonic sanshin fragments, flat-seventh color, short semitone and whole-tone slides.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Skin Pickup** — sanshin attacks land one swung subdivision before machine accents and pull the groove forward without moving the programmed beat.
2. **Clap Shadow** — selected programmed claps are replaced by muted sanshin rakes or dead-string snaps.
3. **Slide Lock** — a fretless slide begins before the beat and reaches its target exactly on the snare or bass arrival.
4. **Three-String Relay** — low, middle, and high sanshin strings carry separate bass-pickup, harmony-color, and offbeat-response roles.
5. **Body Break** — keys and synth bass disappear while drums and sanshin alone maintain the swingbeat identity.
6. **No-Pasted-Folk Rule** — the sanshin cannot be used merely for intros, solos, or decorative pentatonic fills; its physical attack must determine groove timing.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through harder plectrum attack, denser Skin Pickups, brighter claps, more active bass anticipations, stronger vocal doubles, and more frequent Slide Locks. Climaxes remain tight and dance-oriented rather than huge.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Skin-Swing R&B formed inside a converted ferry-ticket annex near Naha harbor. The building retained cream tile, old fare windows, green desk lamps, route stamps, metal drawer units, timetable boards, rubber floor mats, ticket punches, and wide windows facing ferry ramps and humid winter light.

Sanshin player **Emi Arakaki** had been using the annex because its dry interior made the difference between plectrum attack and snakeskin body resonance unusually clear. Her background crossed traditional Okinawan repertoire, singer accompaniment, and contemporary ensemble work, but her interest centered on articulation rather than ornament.

Newark producer **Marcus Vale** arrived with a drum sampler, rack synth, FM electric-piano module, and a set of unfinished swingbeat demos. His first session placed sanshin melody over a complete programmed rhythm and sounded superficial. During editing he muted the clap by accident and heard Emi’s hard rake fill nearly the same space. Instead of restoring the clap, he rebuilt the whole pattern around the acoustic strike.

Singer and keyboardist **Maya Foster** joined after developing short vocal hooks that left room for Skin Pickups. Bassist and programmer **Riku Higa** shaped short synth-bass phrases around Emi’s attacks. Bass-clarinetist **Darius Coleman** completed the ensemble with low acoustic punctuation for Body Breaks and sparse bridges.

Their first performances were called **fare windows**. Three narrow lights behind Emi were labeled LOW, MID, and HIGH to indicate which sanshin string carried the dominant rhythmic role in each section. The lights controlled nothing. They simply made the instrument’s internal routing visible.

The visual language developed around lacquer black, ferry-terminal cream, ticket-stamp red, sea-glass green, snakeskin grey, brushed aluminum, faded teal, and warm fluorescent white. Wardrobe favors practical contemporary clothes—loose shirts, pleated trousers, knit tops, jackets, simple dresses, sneakers—rather than ceremonial costume or retro-1989 cosplay.

The unofficial rule is that if the sanshin can be muted and the drum pattern still feels exactly the same, the arrangement has failed.

